    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u explain the difference between high glycemic index foods and low glycemic index foods? also why does it matter for insulin?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "High glycemic index (GI) foods rapidly raise blood sugar because they are broken down quickly, leading to a sharp spike in blood glucose. Conversely, low GI foods are digested slowly, causing a more gradual and sustained rise in blood sugar. This matters for insulin because a rapid spike forces the pancreas to release a large amount of insulin, which can contribute to insulin resistance over time. Choosing low GI options helps keep blood sugar levels more stable, reducing the strain on your body's insulin response.",
      "source": "target",
